@elezar I don't see how https://github.com/cncf-tags/container-device-interface/pull/371 would help in this case? Distrobox and Toolbx use `--userns=keep-id` podman argument which will add a custom uid AND gid mapping to map the current user/group id to the same user/group id in the container.
A spec for that will always have both mappings, like on my system for example:
```
"uidMappings": [
      {
        "containerID": 0,
        "hostID": 1,
        "size": 1000
      },
      {
        "containerID": 1000,
        "hostID": 0,
        "size": 1
      },
      {
        "containerID": 1001,
        "hostID": 1001,
        "size": 64536
      }
    ],
    "gidMappings": [
      {
        "containerID": 0,
        "hostID": 1,
        "size": 1000
      },
      {
        "containerID": 1000,
        "hostID": 0,
        "size": 1
      },
      {
        "containerID": 1001,
        "hostID": 1001,
        "size": 64536
      }
    ],
```

The problem is that cdi assumes idmapped mounts are available without knowing if they are even support in then env. They will never work with rootless podman because we do not run in the initial user namespace and do not have any special privileges to perfom them. The same will go for any runtime not running in the initial user namespace.
